 **Sprint summary.** Seven tissue and plasma validations (VAL-058 through VAL-064) all PASS at the prereg-sealed Cohen's d ≥ 0.5 threshold, plus one exploratory urine-arm specimen comparison (VAL-065), plus the pancreatic-epic v0.1 build (VAL-066/067/068/069) which delivered three independent tissue cohorts plus a directional Xu-538 fallback panel. Two framework-relevant stratification findings emerged: (1) **smoking stratification is mandatory for lung-epic** — TCGA-LUAD is 76% ever-smoker and the never-smoker arm (n=2) is underpowered for independent inference. (2) **Chronic viral hepatitis blunts the paired tumor-vs-adjacent-normal contrast in HCC** through methylation drift in the adjacent-normal liver tissue ("field defect" per Villanueva 2015), shrinking the paired contrast even though the tumor architecture is genuinely disrupted. Non-viral HCC (alcohol/NAFLD) shows classical secretory-class magnitude (d ≈ +0.66, comparable to breast secretory). The pattern parallels the lung smoking finding and may generalize: chronic disease-driver exposures drive adjacent-normal field defects that need to be controlled for in paired-tissue analyses. **VAL-065 is exploratory:** GSE119260 (Brikun 2018) is the only public EPIC 850K urine methylation prostate cohort and contains only n=4 advanced-disease patients (all bone metastatic, Gleason 4+4 to 5+5). Sample size precludes substrate-vs-substrate inference. The observed urine vs benign tissue paired d = −2.39 (urine A-score dramatically lower than benign tissue in all 4 patients) was anticipated by the pre-registration as O5_UNEXPECTED and is documented as an open question pending a larger urine methylation prostate cohort with healthy controls and mixed disease stages.
 
 **Pancreatic-epic build (VAL-066/067/068/069).** Three findings emerged: (1) PDAC is the **second confirmed bidirectional-cancellation disease** at the Xu-538 panel level (the first was AD via VAL-050/051). Per-CpG positive-direction percentages cluster at 50% across three independent tissue cohorts (46.9% / 50.4% / 52.9%); pooled-entropy A-score CIs span zero in all three. (2) The directional fallback approach generalized in CCL-027 — frozen ±1 directions per CpG with z-score normalization — recovers the signal cleanly on the TCGA-PAAD holdout (paired d = +1.51, p = 6.4e-05, all 7 patients positive) and partially on GSE74071 (4 of 7 pairs strongly positive, single pair PH64 outlier ΔA_dir = −1.17 drags the mean). (3) **A universal Stage 1 rule was clarified across the framework via panc-LL-007**: Stage 1 ALWAYS scores Xu-538 against H_min(immune) = 0.838889 regardless of which disease card is being run. Earlier drafts of all four VAL studies erroneously used H_min(secretory); Cohen's d unchanged scale-invariant but absolute A-scores were 0.5% off and would have broken cross-card numerical comparability. Panel-class governs H_min in Stage 1; tissue-class is a Stage 2 concept only. The pancreatic-epic card is anchored at `cohort_screening_validated` by VAL-046 (Rotterdam pre-dx blood n=182) with a `tissue_arm_exploratory_with_directional_recovery_partial` modifier from VAL-066/067/068/069.
 
+**Cervical-epic build (VAL-071/072/073/074/076/077/081).** Six VAL studies on total n=807 samples scored across every publicly-accessible 450K/EPIC cervical methylation cohort per CCL-029 cohort-completeness. Three findings emerged: (1) **Tissue-arm cohort heterogeneity surfaces only at full breadth.** VAL-073 GSE99511 Verlaat (Amsterdam, n=68) provides a clean tissue anchor at d = +0.73 monotonic Normal<CIN3<SCC. VAL-074 GSE46306 Farkas (Stockholm, n=43) reads NEGATIVE-direction at d = −0.61 with HPV-negative healthy normals shifting baseline. VAL-081 GSE68339 Lando (Oslo, n=270 SCC tumors) reads tumors d = −0.43 below VAL-073 normals as external comparator at large n. Two of three tissue cohorts at total n=313 read negative-direction; **VAL-073 is the outlier, not the artifact**. The cohort-direction-flip is real biological/cohort-design heterogeneity (most likely HPV-stratification of normals), not a measurement artifact. With single-cohort validation (VAL-073 alone) the card would have published at single_cohort_validated tier; CCL-029 caught the heterogeneity. (2) **LBC primary pathway requires panel engineering.** VAL-076 El-Zein (Quebec, n=186 LBC EPIC 850K) reads flat across CIN grades on raw β; the Xu-538 panel was buffy-coat trained, LBC samples are ~80% exfoliated cervical epithelium + ~10-20% mucosal-resident lymphocytes (different cell mixture). The published clinical-grade cervical methylation panels (FAM19A4/miR124-2 [QIAsure AUC 0.77], ZNF671 [GynTect], EPB41L3, PAX1/NREP-AS1 [Bowden 2025 AUC 0.92]) detect strong signal in the same data products where the universal Stage 1 panel reads null — that is a panel-transferability finding, not a "no signal" finding. VAL-077 Bowden (Imperial London, n=247 LBC EPIC 850K) was deferred to v0.2+ after CHK-3.1 distribution check revealed the supplementary file is residual M-values per Bowden 2025 Methods, NOT raw β. (3) **CCL-032 was promoted to absolute rule on April 25** based on this build: diagnostic order is fixed: data integrity → biology → framework. Never the reverse. The cervical-epic v0.1 record is the proof-of-value for the rule — initial outcome drafts treated null framework readings as biology before checking data integrity or biology consistency; both VAL-076 and VAL-077 were reclassified from O3_NULL to O6_UNEXPECTED after CCL-032 was applied. cervical-epic v0.1 publishes at the new `exploratory_with_cohort_heterogeneity` tier (added to master README v2.1 specifically for the cohort-direction-flip pattern); path to v0.2+ documented in the card README §17 includes raw IDAT reprocessing of GSE287994, HPV-stratified re-run of all tissue cohorts, cervical-LBC-specific panel engineering or substitution of published clinical-grade panels with dedicated H_min calibration, and Test 2 lymphoid/myeloid sub-panel split when OQ-2026-01 immune-atlas staging operationalizes.
+
 ### Healthy baseline reference tables (8 classes × 10 age decades, 80 cells) — April 18, 2026
 
 Companion to the cascade: per-age-decade expected healthy A-score for every architecture class. A patient A-score above the age-matched p90 is above 90% of the healthy population at that age; combined with the tier thresholds (MARGINAL ≥ 1.01, DETECTABLE ≥ 1.05, URGENT ≥ 1.07, FLOOR BREACH ≥ 1.10), this provides a two-axis clinical readout (age-percentile × tier).
